-capture/14066605/assets/8fb9e432fe79c363a42d362ef5c352eab6e8b55b83583f2a946ed9251ce293e3.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x).$ Find $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $1.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1,2}=\\pm 1,$ and ${x}_{3,4}=\\pm 4\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=1.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =1(x-1)(x-(-1))(x-(4\\text{i}))(x-(-4\\text{i})) \\\\  & =1(x-1)(x+1)(x-4\\text{i})(x+4\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1)(x-4\\text{i})(x+4\\text{i}) & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+x-x-{1}^{2})({x}^{2}+4\\text{i}x-4\\text{i}x-(4\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+16) & = \\\\ {x}^{4}+16{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}-16 & = \\\\ {x}^{4}+15{x}^{2}-16 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)={x}^{4}+15{x}^{2}-16.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-8212/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eda5034-e8a8-5260-8dc0-6c663880fe9e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-8212"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8801"] :question/problem "The quartic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots $x_1=0, x_2=-1,x_3=2$ and $x_4=-3,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $4.$ Find $P(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since our quartic polynomial has roots ${x_1=0, x_2=-1},$ ${x_3=2},$ and ${x_4=-3},$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is ${a=4},$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) & =4(x-0)(x-(-1))(x-2)(x-(-3)) \\\\  & =4(x-0)(x+1)(x-2)(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-0)(x+1)(x-2)(x+3) & =4({x}^{2}+x)({x}^{2}+x-6) \\\\  & =4({x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-6x) \\\\  & =4({x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-6x) \\\\  & =4{x}^{4}+8{x}^{3}-20{x}^{2}-24x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x) = 4x^4+8x^3-20x^2-24x.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5796"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066605/assets/658fa5a494ef01b5924847335ce863aa8c6b5f8adee5ee97967a8c8bebd83a80.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x).$ Find $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $1.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x_1=-2},$ ${x_2=3},$ and ${x_{3,4}=1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the quartic term is ${a=1}.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (x-(-2))(x-(3))(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x-3)(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-3)(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i})) & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-3x+2x-6)({x}^{2}-x(1-3\\text{i})-x(1+3\\text{i})+(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i})) & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x-6)({x}^{2}-2x+10) & = \\\\ {x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-10x-6{x}^{2}+12x-60 & = \\\\ {x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+2x-60 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x) = x^4 - 3x^3 + 6x^2 + 2x - 60.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
